This is the classic maximum sum bitonic subsequence problem. Compute the maximum-sum increasing subsequence ending at each index (left to right), and the maximum-sum decreasing subsequence starting at each index (right to left). The answer is the maximum over i of `inc[i] + dec[i] - arr[i]`, since arr[i] is counted in both.

Check with the test: for [1, 15, 51, 45, 33, 100, 12, 18, 9], the best is 1 + 15 + 51 + 100 + 18 + 9 = 194.

```python
def max_sum(arr):
    n = len(arr)
    if n == 0:
        return 0
    inc = arr[:]
    for i in range(n):
        for j in range(i):
            if arr[j] < arr[i] and inc[j] + arr[i] > inc[i]:
                inc[i] = inc[j] + arr[i]
    dec = arr[:]
    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):
        for j in range(n - 1, i, -1):
            if arr[j] < arr[i] and dec[j] + arr[i] > dec[i]:
                dec[i] = dec[j] + arr[i]
    return max(inc[i] + dec[i] - arr[i] for i in range(n))
```

